# Nothing merges on a hunch

Topic 12, talk 1

After this talk you can:

- run an engine headless and collect results per battle
- report a mean with a 95% interval, and say what overlap means
- decide a change with a paired A/B on the same seeds

---

## Why a bench at all

Hadur 1.x was judged by watching battles. There was no way to tell a gain from noise.

S0 built a bench and measured 1.20 for the first time. The finding: Hadur wins about half its rounds against Shadow (82 of 175) but takes only 40.8% of the score, because Shadow deals more bullet damage (36.5% damage share) even in rounds Shadow loses.

So the headline metric became score share, not rounds won.

Source: `docs/strategy-evolution.md`, S0.

---

## One JVM per battle, one seed per battle

```java
cmd.add(javaBin);
cmd.addAll(JVM_FLAGS);
cmd.add("-DRANDOMSEED=" + seed);
cmd.add("-cp");
cmd.add(cp);
cmd.add(BattleRunner.class.getName());
// ... then home, dir, rounds, width, height, our robot, the opponent
Process p = new ProcessBuilder(cmd)
    .redirectErrorStream(true)
    .redirectOutput(dir.resolve("engine.log").toFile())
    .start();
```

(`Bench.runBattle`.) Robocode's `RANDOMSEED` property makes a battle repeatable: the same command gives the same result. A child process isolates one battle from the next and can be killed after 30 minutes.

---

## Headless means the control API

`robocode.control` runs a battle from code. No window.

```java
RobocodeEngine engine = new RobocodeEngine(home);
engine.addBattleListener(new BattleAdaptor() {
    @Override
    public void onBattleCompleted(BattleCompletedEvent e) {
        for (BattleResults r : e.getSortedResults()) { /* ... */ }
    }
});
RobotSpecification[] robots =
    engine.getLocalRepository(us + "," + them);
engine.runBattle(new BattleSpecification(rounds,
    new BattlefieldSpecification(width, height), robots), true);
```

(`BattleRunner`.) The listener is an anonymous subclass of an adapter: override only what you need.

---

## A mean and an interval

One battle is noisy. Five seeds give a mean and a 95% interval (Student's t, because n is small).

```java
double mean = sum / n;
double sd = Math.sqrt(ss / (n - 1));
double t = n - 1 < T975.length ? T975[n - 1] : 1.96;
return new Stats(n, mean, t * sd / Math.sqrt(n));
```

(`Stats.of`.) With 5 seeds, t is 2.776, not 1.96. Using the normal value would make every interval too narrow.

The report prints `57.4% ± 5.0`: the mean and the half-width.

---

## Reading an interval

Cold bench against Shadow 3.83c, 35 rounds by 5 seeds:

| Stage | Score share |
|---|---|
| S0 (1.20) | 40.8% ± 8.3 |
| S1 | 46.2% ± 6.4 |
| S5 | 46.3% ± 4.5 |
| S6 | 57.4% ± 5.0 |

S0 and S1 overlap: within the noise, as S1 intended (it changed no play). S5 and S6 do not overlap: S6 is the first stage to move the score outside the noise.

Source: `docs/strategy-evolution.md`, "Results by stage".

---

## Noise is bigger than you think

From the learnings ledger (L-19):

- single opponents move 3 to 14 points between 5 by 35 runs
- so per-bot changes under about 8 points are noise
- compare at 10 seeds, or use the paired A/B
- running `mvn` during a bench inflates skipped turns

In M6 a weight sweep was run at 10 seeds because the same jar gave 51.3 and 53.7 on 5 seeds earlier. Nothing was compared at fewer than 10.

---

## Pair the seeds (BENCH-2)

The candidate and the baseline fight the same opponent on the same seed. Subtract per seed, then take the mean and interval of the differences.

```java
public static Stats pairedDiff(List<Double> candidate,
                               List<Double> baseline) {
    int n = Math.min(candidate.size(), baseline.size());
    List<Double> diffs = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        diffs.add(candidate.get(i) - baseline.get(i));
    }
    return of(diffs);
}
```

Whatever the seed did to both (a lucky opening, a hard field) cancels. If the interval of the differences excludes 0, the change is real.

---

## Gates, written down

Each stage names a gate before it starts, and the bench report says whether it was met.

S6's gate had two halves.

- Score outside the noise: met (57.4% ± 5.0).
- Zero skipped turns: not met. 72 over the whole bench, 26 against Shadow.

The report says so. The next thing to try is named (move the checkpoint write off the round's last turn). A gate that fails is evidence too.

M6's gate, reference APS 60, was also not met: 52.7 to 54.6.

---

## Ground truth makes fixes specific

The bench logs both robots' true positions, energy and live bullets every turn. Later stages are scored against what really happened.

- Waves found vs the engine's real bullets: S2 turned "the surf is a bit off" into three specific fixes.
- Bullet shadows: every one of the 927 enemy bullets that Hadur's own bullets destroyed in the S6 cold bench fell inside a shadow Hadur had computed.

When a check says 35%, then 83%, then 100%, you know you are converging.

---

## Conditions are part of the result

Cold: data wiped before every battle. Warm: kept across consecutive battles. Client conditions (`--client`): a forced CPU constant, a shared data directory, background load. Session: 300 battles in one engine process.

Every report says which. A number without its conditions is half a number.

Next talk: does any of this predict the live rumble?

